{"hash":"810e339a7c82fba3","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":94.576,"said":"Stop here, beside the wall, and look at the stones. See how big each block is, and how evenly it is cut. The temple rises about eighty feet, built of great grey slabs of local stone, raised on a broad rectangular platform. Even the temple committee says these slabs make you wonder how anyone moved and set them, up here, so far from any road. Accounts of the temple describe the blocks as locked to one another with iron clamps, with no mortar between them. Who built it, and when? No one knows for certain. The temple committee holds that Adi Shankaracharya built it in the eighth century; older tradition says the Pandavas raised the first shrine here. Since then it has stood through earthquakes, and through half of every year buried in snow. After the flood of 2013, engineers from IIT Madras, asked by the Archaeological Survey of India, tested the walls and the foundation with instruments that do not harm the stone. Their finding: no serious danger. The small settlements they found were centuries old, from when the temple was built, not from the flood. So the wonder here is real: a house of stone made by human hands, that these mountains have not managed to break. Walk on round the back. A rock that stood beside it in 2013 is waiting.\n\nDid you know? The temple committee says the stones of Kedarnath are local, cut and carved here.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1250},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1266,"end":1347}],"words":[[0.098,0.421,0,4],[0.531,0.349,5,9],[1.12,0.457,11,17],[1.588,0.108,18,21],[1.709,0.385,22,26],[2.274,0.18,28,31],[2.466,0.192,32,36],[2.67,0.12,37,39],[2.802,0.096,40,43],[2.911,0.613,44,50],[4.399,0.288,52,55],[4.699,0.216,56,59],[4.928,0.312,60,63],[5.252,0.252,64,68],[5.517,0.325,69,74],[5.853,0.3,75,77],[6.298,0.144,79,82],[6.454,0.312,83,86],[6.779,0.457,87,93],[7.248,0.144,94,96],[7.404,0.144,97,99],[7.56,0.373,100,103],[8.834,0.12,105